// WS500-OpenFW — Renode platform description for the STM32F072RB target.
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
//
// Deliverable #19 / issue #25 / PROJECT_PLAN §8.2 (whole-firmware emulation).
//
// This is a thin overlay on Renode's stock STM32F072 CPU platform. That stock
// file (platforms/cpus/stm32f072.repl, which itself pulls in stm32f0.repl)
// already provides everything the WS500 firmware touches during init + loop:
//
//   rcc      Python.PythonPeripheral     @ 0x40021000   (overridden below —
//                                                       GH#38 RCC note)
//   gpioA-F  GPIOPort.STM32_GPIOPort     @ 0x48000000..
//   timer1   Timers.STM32_Timer          @ 0x40012C00   (field PWM, TIM1)
//   timer2   Timers.STM32_Timer          @ 0x40000000   (free-running timebase)
//   timer7   Timers.STM32_Timer          @ 0x40001400
//   adc      Analog.STM32F0_ADC          @ 0x40012400   (+ DMA, dmaChannel 1)
//   dma      DMA.STM32G0DMA              @ 0x40020000
//   i2c1/2   I2C.STM32F7_I2C             @ 0x40005400 / 0x40005800 (INA226/EEPROM)
//   can      CAN.STMCAN                  @ 0x40006400   (bxCAN, PB8/9)
//   exti     IRQControllers.STM32F4_EXTI @ 0x40010400   (stator pulse PA10)
//   Tags:    IWDG, USB, CRS, PWR, SYSCFGCOMP, DBGMCU ... (silent, loggable)
//
// The stock stm32f072.repl also fixes flash=128KB@0x08000000 and sram=16KB
// @0x20000000, matching STM32F072xB_FLASH.ld exactly. (Note the linker script's
// _estack is NOT the top of SRAM: PROJECT_PLAN §7 R4 moved the stack to the
// BOTTOM of RAM so an overflow faults into unmapped space instead of eating
// .bss, so _estack = 0x20000000 + _Min_Stack_Size. Nothing in the platform
// depends on its value — the reset vector supplies it.)
//
// `using` targets are resolved against Renode's install directory (default
// Monitor PATH), so this reference works for both `renode` and `renode-test`.
using "platforms/cpus/stm32f072.repl"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STOCK-PLATFORM BUG WORKAROUND — RCC (GH#38: SYSCLK now HSE->PLLx6, was
// HSI48+CRS). Read this before touching board_clock_config()'s clock init.
//
// CORRECTION to earlier bring-up notes: this file and renode/README.md
// previously described the base platform's `rcc` peripheral as
// `Miscellaneous.STM32F0_RCC`, a compiled model mirroring HSERDY<-HSEON /
// PLLRDY<-PLLON / SWS<-SW. That description does not match Renode 1.16.1's
// actually-installed platforms/cpus/stm32f0.repl: `STM32F0_RCC` does not
// exist anywhere in this release (confirmed by grepping the whole `platforms/`
// tree — only F4/L0/H7/WBA get a compiled RCC model; F0 and G0 get a generic
// Python "flip-flop" placeholder). Its actual script, verbatim:
//
//     if request.IsInit: lastVal = 0
//     elif request.IsRead:
//         lastVal = 1 - lastVal
//         if Offset == 0x0:  Value = lastVal * 0x83          # CR
//         elif Offset == 0x4: Value = lastVal * 0xFFFFFFF8   # CFGR
//         elif Offset == 0x24: Value = 0x0                   # CSR
//         else: Value = lastVal * 0xFFFFFFFF                 # everything else
//     # IsWrite is not handled at all -- every write is silently discarded.
//
// Every write to CR (HSEON, PLLON, HSI48ON via CR2's "everything else" arm)
// is a no-op, and CR's read value alternates between 0x83 and 0x00 on every
// single read regardless — HSERDY (bit 17) and PLLRDY (bit 25) can NEVER be
// observed set, full stop, not just "unlikely": those bits are not part of
// either alternated value. This was confirmed two ways during GH#38: (1)
// reading the actual installed script above, and (2) direct `sysbus
// ReadDoubleWord`/`WriteDoubleWord` probing against this exact platform,
// whose noisy alternating 0x83/0x00 readings (with no correlation to the
// writes in between) is exactly what the flip-flop predicts and was the clue
// that led to reading the script in the first place.
//
// CONSEQUENCE FOR OUR FIRMWARE: HAL_RCC_OscConfig(HSE) will never see HSERDY,
// so board_clock_config()'s bounded ~100 ms HSE_STARTUP_TIMEOUT wait (see
// board.c) fires on every single emulated boot, and the firmware falls back
// to HSI48 — which is NOT hung (the fallback is the whole point of GH#38's
// bounded-wait design; a CI job that only ever exercises the fallback branch
// still stays green), but it does mean the primary HSE path would get ZERO
// coverage from `emulation` if left alone: every run would silently take the
// same branch, forever, and a real regression in the HSE/PLL configuration
// itself would never be caught here.
//
// FIX: override the peripheral's `script` property (a same-name, same-type
// re-set — the DMA1 override below already establishes this is legal;
// REDECLARING `rcc` with a different Renode type is what fails to load with
// "Variable already declared", per that section) with a real store-and-return
// model, narrowly scoped to exactly what Core/Src/board.c's
// board_clock_config() touches: CR ready-bit mirroring (HSIRDY<-HSION,
// HSERDY<-HSEON, PLLRDY<-PLLON), CFGR SWS<-SW (HAL_RCC_ClockConfig's switch-
// wait loop polls this), and CR2 ready-bit mirroring (HSI14RDY<-HSI14ON,
// HSI48RDY<-HSI48ON). Deliberately NOT modelled: CFGR2 PREDIV / CFGR PLLMUL
// readback, so HAL_RCC_GetSysClockFreq()'s derived Hz value (used only to
// reprogram SysTick's reload via HAL_InitTick, called at the end of every
// HAL_RCC_ClockConfig) is not guaranteed exact under this stub — no worse
// than the flip-flop it replaces, and the four existing CI cases already
// pass against generous (5-30 s) Log Tester budgets, not tight real-time
// ones. Every OTHER offset (CSR, AHBENR, APB1/2ENR, CFGR2, CFGR3, ...) falls
// through to a plain store-and-return, same as before but at least
// internally consistent (a write followed by a read now returns what was
// written, instead of an unrelated alternating value) — nothing in this
// firmware polls those back, so this is a strict improvement with no
// observed way for it to regress the existing liveness/fault/IWDG/I2C cases.
//
// State does NOT reset across a software reset (SYSRESETREQ, the §7 R3 fault
// test and the IWDG-starvation test both trigger one): Renode does not
// re-issue IsInit to a PythonPeripheral just because the CPU core reset, so
// a re-boot's board_clock_config() sees HSEON/PLLON already "1" from before
// the reset and its own HSERDY/PLLRDY reads come back set immediately. Real
// silicon does clear RCC to POR defaults on this path, so this stub is looser
// than hardware here — but no existing test depends on RCC state actually
// resetting, and a faster-than-hardware clock bring-up on the SECOND boot
// cannot itself cause a timeout that a instantaneous bring-up avoids.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
rcc:
    script: '''
if request.IsInit:
    regs = {0x00: 0x83}   # POR reset: HSION|HSIRDY|HSITRIM default (real
                          # STM32F0 CR reset value, confirmed against V6's
                          # rcc_v6.py trace notes)
elif request.IsRead:
    v = regs.get(request.Offset, 0)
    if request.Offset == 0x00:              # CR
        if v & (1 << 0):  v |= (1 << 1)     # HSIRDY  <- HSION
        if v & (1 << 16): v |= (1 << 17)    # HSERDY  <- HSEON
        if v & (1 << 24): v |= (1 << 25)    # PLLRDY  <- PLLON
    elif request.Offset == 0x04:            # CFGR
        v = (v & ~0xC) | ((v & 0x3) << 2)   # SWS <- SW
    elif request.Offset == 0x34:            # CR2
        if v & (1 << 0):  v |= (1 << 1)     # HSI14RDY <- HSI14ON
        if v & (1 << 16): v |= (1 << 17)    # HSI48RDY <- HSI48ON
    request.Value = v
elif request.IsWrite:
    regs[request.Offset] = request.Value
'''
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// KNOWN GAP — no BOOT0 flash mirror at 0x00000000.
//
// The stock stm32f072.repl registers `flash` at 0x08000000 only. Real hardware
// with BOOT0=0 also mirrors it at 0x00000000, and on a Cortex-M0 (no VTOR)
// that mirror is where the CPU fetches the initial SP/PC after EVERY reset —
// including the software reset our §7 R3 fault path performs. Verified against
// Renode 1.16.1: without the mirror the CPU halts with "PC does not lay in
// memory" the moment SYSRESETREQ lands.
//
// It is NOT added here, deliberately: changing the shared platform would touch
// the existing CI liveness test as well. The fault test in ws500.robot
// installs it per-machine instead (`sysbus Unregister sysbus.flash` + a
// re-registration at both addresses). Fold it in here if a second consumer
// ever needs it.
//
// Note `sysbus Redirect 0x0 0x08000000 0x20000` is NOT a substitute: the
// redirected region serves data reads but cannot be executed from, so vector
// fetches through it return 0.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STOCK-PLATFORM BUG WORKAROUND — DMA1.
//
// Renode 1.16.1's platforms/cpus/stm32f0.repl models DMA1 as a
// Python.PythonPeripheral whose write path is broken:
//
//     if request.IsWrite and request.Offset in (0x10, 0x24, 0x38):
//         sysbus.WriteDoubleWord(request.Value, 0x40E33333)
//
// `sysbus` is not bound inside a PythonPeripheral script, so ANY write to a
// channel's CPAR (peripheral-address) register raises "name 'sysbus' is not
// defined" and takes the whole Renode process down — not a test failure, a
// crash.
//
// Nothing hit it until 2026-07-27, because the firmware never actually
// configured DMA: HAL_ADC_MspInit did not exist, so hdma_adc1 was never
// initialised and HAL_ADC_Start_DMA() bailed out early. Fixing that real
// hardware bug is what first drove a CPAR write onto the emulated bus — the
// emulation had been silently green over a path it never executed.
//
// Fixed by overriding the offending script (a .repl overlay may re-set a
// property of an existing entry, but may NOT redeclare the name with a
// different type — swapping in the real DMA.STM32G0DMA model here fails to
// load with "Variable 'DMA' was already declared", verified against Renode
// 1.16.1 locally). The replacement drops the sysbus poke entirely and keeps
// the peripheral a silent, loggable register sink.
//
// Dropping rather than repairing the write is deliberate: its purpose was to
// force a nonzero value into the DMA's target buffer for firmwares that spin
// waiting on output. This firmware does not — sensors.c polls a circular
// buffer and is content with zeros — so "repairing" it would only scribble
// 0x40E33333 into the middle of the ADC scan results and invent sensor
// readings that never happened.
//
// Consequence, stated plainly: DMA remains UNMODELLED here, so the ADC scan
// buffer reads as zeros under emulation and the §0.6 V4 scaling path is still
// bench-only (test-fw's `adc` command, deliverable #14, is that step). What
// this change buys is that the emulation no longer dies when the firmware
// configures DMA correctly. If real DMA behaviour is ever wanted, the route
// is a `sysbus Unregister sysbus.DMA` + re-registration in the .resc/.robot,
// the same trick the fault test already uses for the BOOT0 flash mirror.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DMA:
    script: '''
if request.IsWrite:
    self.NoisyLog("DMA write 0x%x at offset 0x%x (unmodelled, discarded)"
                  % (request.Value, request.Offset))
'''

// KNOWN GAP — IWDG is a silent Tag region (see the Tag list above), so the
// §7 R1 watchdog cannot be exercised here: key-register writes are logged and
// discarded, and no reset is ever generated. What that costs, and what covers
// it instead, is written up in renode/README.md.

// No INA226 model exists in Renode. The firmware's ina2xx.c uses HAL_I2C_Mem_*
// with a fixed 5 ms per-transaction timeout ("never an unbounded wait/loop"),
// so an unpopulated I2C1 bus NACKs/times out without stalling the main loop.
// To make sensor reads return real data once a model is available, attach it
// in the .resc via LoadPlatformDescriptionFromString "... @ i2c1 0x40".
